A||||3||Groot||Alternator Wiring Messed Up
Z||000000||Groot||08-24-99||03:14 AM||||Hi,<p>I have problems with my alternator. It charges with 17 Volts !! Far too high.<br>A closer look to the wiring reveals that<br>someone has been messing it up.<p>I have a 84 fiero 2.5L 4 cyl. and the<br>alternator has one big red top-wire and<br>a connector with two wires ( red - black )<p>Where are these wires supposed to go to.<br>The red top wire ( with the rubber cap )<br>does not go to the 3-point on the battery ?<p>Does someone have info on how to rewire<br>this properly ?<p>( Is the regulator built in or external ? )
Z||000001||Patrick||08-24-99||03:26 PM||||<br>Egbert, I'm glad to see that you posted at this site again. As I said in response to your email to me, you'll probably get a better answer about this from somebody else here other than me.<IMG SRC="/ubb/smile.gif" WIDTH=15 HEIGHT=15><p>The <b>thick</b> red wire goes to some kind of a junction near the battery and then in turn is connected to the positive of the battery. I don't know what you are referring to by a "3-point on the battery".<p>As far as I know, alternators on all Fieros have internal regulators. According to my Haynes manual, the brown wire goes to the "idiot" light in the instrument panel, and the red wire from the regulator goes to the positive of the battery. Keep in mind that as with the <b>thick</b> red wire, these two wires from the regulator may not go DIRECTLY to their destination. They may go to some kind of a junction also.<p>It might be easier in the long run to simply remove your alternator and have it tested in a shop. It takes them about two minutes to set it up, and normally they will NOT charge you anything for this service.<p>Your wiring may have been tampered with, but that does not necessarily mean that the wiring is responsible for your charging system not working properly. My guess is that it's your regulator that's toast.<p><p><br>
Z||000002||DJRice||08-25-99||07:18 AM||||As a warning, if you do take it to a parts place to be tested, sometimes they cant translate what their machine tells them.  When I had my alt tested the kid told me "The diode light is on."  I asked him what that meant and he said "The diode light is on."  What that meant was that apparently a diode in the rectifier bridge had failed, and the alternator needed replacing.  <p>The point is that watch the testing machine because sometimes all they know how to do is turn it on.....
Z||000003||Groot||08-26-99||01:54 AM||||Ok, you've got me convinced !<br>I'll take the alternator to a test-bank<br>first !<p>The wires coming from my alt. are BLACK and<br>RED in one connector ( besides the thick red one ). I don't have an idiot lamp ....<p>I'll get back !!<p>Thanks so far,<br>Egbert
